# daripada penyimpanan kontainer lokal. %d saat ini: %s
# `USE_S3_FOR_EXPORTS=false` (templates/env.j2): media (avatar, penutup) Dia
# pergi ke ember objstore umum, tapi ekspor rekening swasta tetap di sistem
# berkas lokal kontainer. Mereka tidak dapat berbagi ember media: model
# objstore memberikan satu ember per aplikasi dan SeaweedFS tidak ada ACL
# per-prefix, jadi tak dikenal dibaca (`public: true`) baldi media baldi juga
# akan mengekspos `exports/`. Perbaikan bersih memerlukan memperpanjang
# `objstore` mencari untuk menyelesaikan kedua, bernama, pribadi (`public:
# false`) baldi baldi per aplikasi ((c.g. `bookwyrm-exports`), menyediakannya
# dengan aplikasi S3 Kunci, dan berikan anon-read hanya pada ember media.
# Kemudian di set `USE_S3_FOR_EXPORTS=true` + + + + + +
# `EXPORTS_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME` Ke ember pribadi itu (BookWyrm ♪ sudah
# digunakan ♪ `default_acl: private` + + + + + + `querystring_auth: True`,
# jadi akses signed-URL terus ekspor swasta)Touches shared objstore infra,
# jadi itu milik sendiri PR bersama konsumen data pribadi lainnya.
# '(', '♪']
msgid "Store user exports in object storage instead of local container storage. Currently `USE_S3_FOR_EXPORTS=false` (templates/env.j2): media (avatars, covers) goes to the public objstore bucket, but private account exports stay on the container's local filesystem. They cannot share the media bucket: the objstore model gives one bucket per app and SeaweedFS enforces no per-prefix ACL, so an anonymous-readable (`public: true`) media bucket would also expose `exports/`. Clean fix requires extending the `objstore` lookup to resolve a second, named, private (`public: false`) bucket per app (e.g. `bookwyrm-exports`), provision it with the app's S3 key, and grant anon-read only on the media bucket. Then set `USE_S3_FOR_EXPORTS=true` + `EXPORTS_STORAGE_BUCKET_NAME` to that private bucket (BookWyrm already uses `default_acl: private` + `querystring_auth: True`, so signed-URL access keeps exports private). Touches shared objstore infra, so it belongs in its own PR alongside other private-data consumers."
msgstr ""

